            heading: 'The Custody of the Dead and the Divine',
            body: 'The queen of the underworld looked at the child and wanted him for herself. When Aphrodite came to reclaim her treasure, Persephone refused to give him back — and what followed was the strangest lawsuit in mythology: love and death, the two powers that rule mortals, at each other’s throats over a mortal of their own. The case went up to Zeus, and Zeus judged as Solomon might have: the year was cut in three. One third of it Adonis would spend with Persephone below; one third with Aphrodite above; and one third was his own, to dispose as he pleased. The judgment pleased no one — and then it pleased Aphrodite very much, for Adonis freely gave his own third to her as well. Two-thirds of every year he spent in the arms of love; only the dark months belonged to Persephone. The queen of the dead, who had so rarely desired anything, lost the one thing she had wanted to keep — and some say it was her jealousy that whispered the end of the story into being.',
